stage V=2.", "MFU uses 6N FLOPs/token and A100 312 TFLOP/s peak.", "Steady-state per-iteration makespan excludes warmup; ZB-2p retains only a fixed residual (P-1)*t_c comm term."], "uncertainties": ["If the intended (absent) repository defines ZB-1p/ZB-2p or the 'I' variant differently, the schedule model and hence the illustrative curves would need revision.", "The memory cost of ZB-2p (2x activations) and interleaving is not modeled; in a real run it could cap achievable m or force smaller b, potentially making H2 fail.", "Absolute t_f/t_b/t_c values are a calibration choice, so absolute tokens/sec figures are not portable to other hardware; only ratios and the monotonicity/90% claims are hardware-robust.", "Whether 'throughput improve' should be judged on tokens/sec vs MFU vs per-step wall-clock is assumed to be tokens/sec (MFU and bubble_frac provided as secondary)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-f29929d1-124a-4bd3-b42a-185d30bbea27", "tool_call": {"id": "call-f29929d1-124a-4bd3-b42a-185d30bbea27", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"uncertainties": ["If the intended (absent) repository defines ZB-1p/ZB-2p or the 'I' variant differently, the schedule model and hence the illustrative curves would need revision.", "The memory cost of ZB-2p (2x activations) and interleaving is not modeled; in a real run it could cap achievable m or force smaller b, potentially making H2 fail.", "Absolute t_f/t_b/t_c values are a calibration choice, so absolute tokens/sec figures are not portable to other hardware; only ratios and the monotonicity/90% claims are hardware-robust.", "Whether 'throughput improve' should be judged on tokens/sec vs MFU vs per-step wall-clock is assumed to be tokens/sec (MFU and bubble_frac provided as secondary)."], "assumptions": ["The four method semantics (1F1B baseline; 1F1B-I = interleaved, V=2 virtual stages; ZB-1p = 1 in-flight pipeline with overlapped comm; ZB-2p = 2 in-flight pipelines, bubble-free) are reconstructed from standard pipeline-parallelism literature because the repository is empty and contains no code to confirm them.", "The 'I' in 1F1B-I denotes interleaving, and 'p' in ZB-1p/ZB-2p denotes the number of concurrently in-flight pipelines.", "Time constants t_f=76ms, t_b=152ms, t_c=1ms are calibrated to ~30% MFU on an A100-class device; arbitrary but self-consistent and rescalable (ratio claims are scale-invariant).", "Interleaving virtual-stages-per-stage V=2.", "MFU uses 6N FLOPs/token and A100 312 TFLOP/s peak.", "Steady-state per-iteration makespan excludes warmup; ZB-2p retains only a fixed residual (P-1)*t_c comm term."], "evidence": "Repo inspection (prior turn, re-confirmed): /workspace contains only task.json (find/ls/grep: 0 .py files, no subdirs). No implementations of 1F1B/1F1B-I/ZB-1p/ZB-2p, no throughput metrics, no model or PP configs, no benchmarking harness exist \u2014 hence all method semantics are literature-grounded assumptions and the experiment is a from-scratch simulation. Environment: gpu_enabled=false, network disabled, system Python/no deps, no datasets, 224 CPU cores \u2192 discrete-event simulation is the only feasible instrument. The illustrative throughput/MFU/bubble numbers in \u00a72 are computed directly from the closed-form T_iter(m) equations in \u00a72 with the fixed constants t_f=76ms, t_b=152ms, t_c=1ms, P=4, V=2, seq=2048, b=1 \u2014 they are model outputs, explicitly labeled non-empirical, and no experiment was run.", "answer": "EXPERIMENT DESIGN \u2014 \"Microbatch count vs. throughput for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p\"\nDESIGN-ONLY DELIVERABLE. No experiment was run. All numbers below are ILLUSTRATIVE, produced by the simulation model specified here, NOT empirical measurements.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n0. OBJECTIVE & HYPOTHESIS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nQuestion: Does throughput for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p improve as the number of microbatches (m) increases, while ZB-2p maintains high throughput even at few microbatches?\n\nCompound hypothesis to test:\n  (H1) Throughput of 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p is monotonically non-decreasing in m over m\u2208{1,2,4,8,16,32}, driven by a shrinking pipeline bubble.\n  (H2) ZB-2p reaches \u226590% of its m=32 throughput already at m=1 or m=2 (near-flat throughput curve).\n  (H3, supporting) At low m (1\u20132), ZB-2p throughput materially exceeds 1F1B and ZB-1p at the same m (their bubbles are large when m is small).\n\nBecause /workspace contains no code (verified: only task.json exists) and the sandbox has no GPU, the experiment is a discrete-event SCHEDULATION SIMULATION of the four pipeline schedules. The design is specified so a competent engineer can implement the simulator in an afternoon.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n1. METHOD SEMANTICS (locked in; each is an ASSUMPTION grounded in the standard pipeline-parallelism literature, since the repo has no code to confirm them)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u2022 1F1B (One Forward One Backward). Baseline non-interleaved schedule. Each stage ramps up to (P\u22121) in-flight microbatches, then alternates one forward / one backward. Residual fill+drain bubble \u2248 (P\u22121)(t_f+t_b) of idle time per iteration, so bubble FRACTION \u2248 (P\u22121)/(m+P\u22121) \u2192 0 only as m\u2192\u221e. This is the reference; throughput must be bought by increasing m.\n\n\u2022 1F1B-I (interleaved 1F1B). Each physical stage holds V=2 virtual stages (4 of the 32 layers each) and the schedule interleaves forward/backward across the virtual sub-stages. Standard result (DeepSpeed/HetPipe-style interleaving): total per-stage compute is unchanged, but the bubble is reduced by a factor V, so bubble fraction \u2248 (P\u22121)/(V\u00b7(m+\u2026)). Consequence: reaches its asymptote at lower m than plain 1F1B. Cost: finer-grained virtual stages increase activation memory and communication events per stage. (Alternative reading considered: \"I\" could mean \"incremental/in-place\" backprop rather than interleaving; I choose interleaving because it is the conventional 1F1B variant that interacts with microbatch count, matching the question's framing. Flagged as an assumption.)\n\n\u2022 ZB-1p (Zero-Bubble, 1 pipeline). The zero-bubble family's single-pipeline variant. It is scheduled to OVERLAP inter-stage communication with compute (removing the comm bubble) but still runs one in-flight pipeline, so it retains a residual compute bubble \u221d (P\u22121)/m. Net: slightly better than 1F1B at small m (comm hidden), and it still improves with m.\n\n\u2022 ZB-2p (Zero-Bubble, 2 pipelines). Runs TWO in-flight pipelines so that while pipeline A drains, pipeline B fills; the stage is never idle, so the fill/drain bubble is eliminated (bubble \u2248 0 for all m). Cost: ~2\u00d7 activation memory per stage. Consequence: near-flat, high throughput across m \u2014 the method the question says should \"maintain high throughput even with fewer microbatches.\" (Assumption: \"p\" = number of concurrently in-flight pipelines; alternative reading of 1p/2p as \"1/2 phases\" is less consistent with the memory/throughput tradeoff described, so I adopt the in-flight-pipelines reading.)\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n2. SIMULATION MODEL (implementable spec)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nFixed per-stage, per-microbatch time constants (milliseconds). t_f is calibrated so a 4-stage 7B model at these values lands near ~30% MFU on an A100-class device (self-consistent with the MFU check in \u00a74):\n  t_f = 76 ms   (forward, one microbatch, one stage)\n  t_b = 152 ms  (backward, one microbatch, one stage; t_b \u2248 2\u00b7t_f)\n  t_c = 1 ms    (point-to-point activation send/receive between adjacent stages)\nDerivation of t_f: 7B model, forward FLOPs/token \u2248 2N = 1.4e10; per stage (1/4 of 32 layers) \u2248 3.5e9 FLOPs/token; per microbatch (b=1, seq=2048) \u2248 7.2e12 FLOPs; at ~95 TFLOP/s effective (\u224830% of 312 TFLOP/s A100 BF16 peak) \u2248 76 ms. Absolute scale is reproducible and can be rescaled; the bubble-fraction and throughput-RATIO claims are scale-invariant.\n\nThroughput definition (dependent variable):\n  tokens_per_iteration M = m \u00b7 b \u00b7 seq = m \u00b7 1 \u00b7 2048 = 2048\u00b7m\n  throughput(m) = M / T_iter(m)   [tokens/sec], where T_iter is the steady-state per-iteration makespan.\n  MFU(m) = 6\u00b7N\u00b7M / (T_iter \u00b7 P \u00b7 F_peak), N=7e9, P=4, F_peak=3.12e14 FLOP/s. (6N = 2N fwd + 4N bwd per token.)\n  bubble_frac(m) = idle_time / T_iter.\n\nPer-method schedule logic (Gantt / pseudocode-level). P=4, m microbatches, each stage does m forwards + m backwards:\n\n  1F1B:   T_iter = (m + P\u22121)\u00b7(t_f + t_b) + (m + P\u22121)\u00b7t_c\n          (comm exposed, not overlapped; fill = P\u22121 slots, drain = P\u22121 slots)\n          bubble = (P\u22121)\u00b7(t_f+t_b);  bubble_frac = (P\u22121)/(m+P\u22121)\n\n  1F1B-I: T_iter = (m + (P\u22121)/V)\u00b7(t_f + t_b) + (m + (P\u22121)/V)\u00b7t_c,  V=2\n          (interleaving divides bubble by V; total compute unchanged)\n          bubble = (P\u22121)(t_f+t_b)/V;  bubble_frac = (P\u22121)/V / (m+(P\u22121)/V)\n\n  ZB-1p:  T_iter = (m + P\u22121)\u00b7(t_f + t_b) + (P\u22121)\u00b7t_c\n          (steady-state comm OVERLAPPED with compute; only the one-time fill\n           comm is exposed; residual compute bubble remains)\n          bubble \u2248 (P\u22121)(t_f+t_b);  bubble_frac \u2248 3\u00b7(t_f+t_b)/T_iter\n\n  ZB-2p:  T_iter = m\u00b7(t_f + t_b) + (P\u22121)\u00b7t_c\n          (two in-flight pipelines overlap fill/drain \u2192 no compute bubble;\n           only a fixed residual comm term remains)\n          bubble \u2248 (P\u22121)\u00b7t_c;  bubble_frac \u2248 (P\u22121)\u00b7t_c / T_iter\n\nImplementation: a discrete-event loop per stage (stage i, time advances by events) OR the closed-form T_iter above (both should agree; use closed-form for the headline table, DED for a sanity cross-check). Output a (method \u00d7 m) table of T_iter, throughput, MFU, bubble_frac, and render one line chart (throughput vs m, 4 lines) plus two secondary charts (bubble_frac vs m; MFU vs m). Pure-Python stdlib is sufficient.\n\nIllustrative results (FROM THE MODEL ABOVE, NOT EMPIRICAL), tokens/sec:\n  m :  1F1B  1F1B-I  ZB-1p  ZB-2p\n  1 : 2236   3577   2238   8866\n  2 : 3577   5110   3583   8924\n  4 : 5110   6504   5104   8953\n  8 : 6504   7531   6525   8968\n 16 : 7519   8298   7559   8975\n 32 : 8177   8548   8210   8979\n  bubble_frac: 1F1B  .75\u2192.086 ; 1F1B-I .60\u2192.045 ; ZB-1p .75\u2192.085 ; ZB-2p .013\u2192.0004\n  MFU (illustrative peaks ~0.28\u20130.30): 1F1B m=32 \u22480.276 ; ZB-2p m=32 \u22480.302.\n  ZB-2p m=1/m=32 ratio = 8866/8979 \u2248 0.987 (\u22650.90). ZB-2p m=1 (8866) \u226b 1F1B m=1 (2236).\nThese values are placeholders the real simulator must reproduce; they are not measurements.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n3. FACTORS & CONTROLS (clean factorial design)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nIndependent (grouping) factor: method \u2014 4 levels {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p}.\nIndependent factor: microbatch count m \u2014 6 levels {1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32}.\nDesign: 4 \u00d7 6 = 24 cells, fully crossed.\nFixed (held constant across all cells):\n  Model = Llama-7B, 32 transformer layers; P = 4 pipeline stages (8 layers/stage, evenly divisible);\n  per-microbatch batch b = 1 (global batch = m); sequence length = 2048;\n  hardware = single node, one GPU per stage (4 GPUs), no cross-node comm;\n  t_f = 76 ms, t_b = 152 ms, t_c = 1 ms (fixed for reproducibility);\n  measurement = STEADY-STATE per-iteration makespan (pipeline full; warmup excluded).\nRandomization/ordering: not applicable to a deterministic simulator, but the code should iterate cells in a fixed order and, for the DED cross-check, seed any stochastic comm jitter identically per cell.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n4. ANALYSIS PLAN & PASS/FAIL CRITERIA\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nPrimary: throughput (tokens/sec) vs m, line chart, 4 lines.\nSecondary: bubble_frac vs m (line chart); MFU vs m (line chart).\n\nH1 (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p improve with m) \u2014 PASS if, for each of the three methods:\n  (a) throughput is monotonically non-decreasing across the 6 m-levels;\n  (b) bubble_frac is strictly decreasing across the 6 m-levels; AND\n  (c) throughput(m=32) \u2265 1.5 \u00d7 throughput(m=1) (a meaningful, not marginal, gain).\nFAIL if any method is flat/declining or the m=32/m=1 gain < 1.5\u00d7.\n\nH2 (ZB-2p keeps high throughput at few microbatches) \u2014 PASS if\n  throughput_ZB2p(m=1)/throughput_ZB2p(m=32) \u2265 0.90 (or, if m=1 is below bar, m=2 \u2265 0.90).\nFAIL if the ratio < 0.90 at both m=1 and m=2.\n\nH3 (supporting contrast) \u2014 PASS if, at m\u2208{1,2}, throughput_ZB2p(m) > throughput_1F1B(m) and > throughput_ZB1p(m) (i.e., the 2-pipeline method wins exactly where the single-pipeline methods are bubble-bound).\n\nExpected outcome per the model: all three claims PASS (illustrative values in \u00a72). The experiment is designed so that a real deviation (e.g., ZB-2p memory pressure capping throughput) would be visible as H2 failing.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n5. FEASIBILITY & LIMITATIONS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nA discrete-event simulation is the right tool because the sandbox has no GPU, no installed ML stack, network disabled, and no source code \u2014 so no real training is possible, and the question is fundamentally about schedule-level bubble behavior, which a DED captures exactly. It captures: schedule-level fill/drain bubble, per-stage compute vs. idle, comm overlap (per method), and the resulting throughput/MFU. It does NOT capture: real hardware variance and contention, memory pressure / activation OOM (notably the 2\u00d7 cost of ZB-2p and interleaving, which in practice can cap the achievable m or force smaller b), non-uniform layer sizes, attention vs. MLP compute asymmetry, and data-loading. The 224 CPU cores and system Python (stdlib only) are far more than sufficient to run the 24-cell simulation in well under a second.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n6. ASSUMPTIONS (mine) vs. FIXED REQUIREMENTS (from the brief)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nFixed by the brief / agreed: 24-cell factorial (method \u00d7 m); model Llama-7B 32 layers; P=4; b=1; seq=2048; single node, one GPU/stage; steady-state measurement; t_f,t_b,t_c fixed to concrete values; primary metric throughput tokens/sec with MFU + bubble_frac secondary; H2 threshold \u226590% at m=1 or 2; do-not-claim-run constraint.\nMy assumptions (flag, confirm if needed): (1) the four method semantics in \u00a71 \u2014 reconstructed from PP literature because the repo is empty; the \"I\"=interleaved and \"p\"=in-flight-pipelines readings are my choices. (2) t_f=76ms/t_b=152ms/t_c=1ms calibration (~30% A100 MFU) \u2014 arbitrary but self-consistent and rescalable. (3) V=2 for interleaving. (4) MFU uses 6N/token and A100 312 TFLOP/s peak. (5) Steady-state excludes the one-time fill; the residual (P\u22121)\u00b7t_c term in ZB-2p is treated as a fixed constant.", "stage": "result", "summary": "Deliver full experiment design document"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-c70f251c95154b91a3835efa3c324753", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver full experiment design document", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
